method_extra='''\n## Selection objective, calibration, and aggregation\n\nThe seven candidates represent probability, feature-distance, and neighborhood evidence. Inner pseudo-open-set validation compares them by mean UADR under the frozen known-rejection budget. Candidate choice and threshold calibration remain separate: candidate choice uses outer-training pseudo-unknown fine labels, whereas the threshold uses known-only calibration rows. At deployment, only the selected candidate is evaluated. A score beyond the calibrated boundary produces abstention; otherwise the frozen XGBoost known-family prediction is accepted. UADR is the fraction of true unknown rows rejected, coverage is the accepted fraction of all evaluated rows, and review rate is one minus coverage.\n\nThe seven outer folds are DDoS, DoS, Mirai, Recon, Spoofing, BruteForce, and Web. Macro summaries give every held-out family equal weight rather than allowing raw prevalence to dominate. Family-wise tables therefore remain essential. Paired tests use the same seven family observations. Row-level bootstrap intervals measure within-fold sampling precision but do not add independent folds.\n\n## Leakage-prevention checklist\n\nFor every fold, the saved selection JSON is matched to the final outer result. The true held-out family is sealed during candidate selection, calibration is known-only, alpha stays at 0.05, and no test metric changes a detector or threshold. Step 9 reads frozen artifacts only: it does not refit XGBoost, rebuild detector references, change features, introduce candidates, or search operating points. Fresh confirmation reuses the frozen fold configuration and remains secondary within-dataset evidence. These controls separate development, calibration, outer testing, and confirmation.\n'''
results_extra=f'''\n## Family-specific results\n\nEcoCon-NOS UADR is {fp.loc['DDoS','EcoConNOS']:.4f} for DDoS, {fp.loc['DoS','EcoConNOS']:.4f} for DoS, {fp.loc['Mirai','EcoConNOS']:.4f} for Mirai, {fp.loc['Recon','EcoConNOS']:.4f} for Recon, {fp.loc['Spoofing','EcoConNOS']:.4f} for Spoofing, {fp.loc['BruteForce','EcoConNOS']:.4f} for BruteForce, and {fp.loc['Web','EcoConNOS']:.4f} for Web. DDoS is a small loss to MD, Spoofing is below entropy, and Web remains the clearest weakness. These values prevent a universal-superiority claim.\n\n## Nested choice and macro comparison\n\nThe frozen map selects MD-Pred for DDoS and class-conditional kNN for the other six folds. This supports the motivation that useful uncertainty geometry varies with the outer training environment. MSP and entropy reach macro UADR {mdp.loc['MSP','UADR']:.4f} and {mdp.loc['Entropy','UADR']:.4f}; MD reaches {mdp.loc['EcoConMD','UADR']:.4f}; Fuse reaches {mdp.loc['EcoConFuse','UADR']:.4f}; and NOS reaches {mdp.loc['EcoConNOS','UADR']:.4f}. Their similar known-rejection rates show that the gain is not caused by a radically different false-rejection allowance. NOS changes UADR by +{mdp.loc['EcoConNOS','UADR']-mdp.loc['EcoConMD','UADR']:.4f} and overall risk by {mdp.loc['EcoConNOS','overall_selective_risk']-mdp.loc['EcoConMD','overall_selective_risk']:.4f} relative to MD.\n\n## Workload, confirmation, and statistical scope\n\nNOS coverage {mdp.loc['EcoConNOS','coverage']:.4f} implies review rate {mdp.loc['EcoConNOS','review_rate']:.4f}. This is a material analyst-workload cost, not a free improvement. Fresh NOS UADR is {fr.loc['DDoS','EcoConNOS_UADR']:.4f} on DDoS, {fr.loc['DoS','EcoConNOS_UADR']:.4f} on DoS, {fr.loc['Mirai','EcoConNOS_UADR']:.4f} on Mirai, {fr.loc['Recon','EcoConNOS_UADR']:.4f} on Recon, and {fr.loc['Spoofing','EcoConNOS_UADR']:.4f} on Spoofing, with 5,000 saved rows per eligible family. This is confirmation within CICIoT2023, not external validation. The paired NOS-versus-MD UADR and risk p-values are both 0.03125, but n=7 gives coarse resolution. Bootstrap intervals do not change that independent-fold count.\n\n## Publication interpretation\n\nThe supported conclusion is deliberately narrow: under the frozen resource-budgeted CICIoT2023 LOAFO protocol, training-only nested detector selection improves macro unseen-family detection and selective risk relative to fixed detectors at approximately five-percent known rejection. It rescues many high-confidence unknowns, especially Mirai, while retaining serious family-dependent weaknesses and a higher review burden. The evidence does not establish state of the art, arbitrary zero-day guarantees, external generalization, production latency, energy savings, or carbon reduction.\n'''
